# Discord Core Operations

Lux provides a shared Discord operation-planning layer for message management,
channel management, moderation, and scheduled events.

## Message Operations

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.Discord.CoreOperations

{:ok, action} =
  CoreOperations.message_action(:create, %{
    channel_id: "123",
    content: "hello",
    idempotency_key: "send:123:request-1"
  })

action.method
action.endpoint
action.payload
```

Execute the plan through the Discord client:

```elixir
Lux.Integrations.Discord.Client.request(action.method, action.endpoint, %{
  json: action.payload
})
```

## Channel Operations

```elixir
CoreOperations.channel_action(:create, %{
  guild_id: "guild-1",
  name: "support",
  type: 0,
  rate_limit_per_user: 10
})

CoreOperations.channel_action(:permission_overwrite, %{
  channel_id: "123",
  overwrite_id: "role-1",
  type: 0,
  allow: "1024",
  deny: "0"
})
```

## Moderation

```elixir
CoreOperations.moderation_action(:timeout, %{
  guild_id: "guild-1",
  user_id: "user-1",
  until: "2026-05-13T12:00:00Z",
  reason: "flood"
})

CoreOperations.moderation_action(:ban, %{
  guild_id: "guild-1",
  user_id: "user-1",
  delete_message_seconds: 3600
})
```

Local warning actions intentionally have `method: :local` and `endpoint: nil`
so callers can persist them without calling Discord.

## Scheduled Events

```elixir
CoreOperations.event_action(:create, %{
  guild_id: "guild-1",
  name: "Office hours",
  scheduled_start_time: "2026-05-13T12:00:00Z",
  privacy_level: 2,
  entity_type: 3
})
```

## Retry Planning

```elixir
CoreOperations.retry_plan({:error, {429, %{"retry_after" => 1.5}}})
#=> %{retry?: true, reason: :rate_limited, delay_ms: 1500}

CoreOperations.retry_plan({:error, {503, "unavailable"}}, %{
  default_delay_ms: 500
})
```

## Operation Events

```elixir
CoreOperations.operation_event(action, :succeeded, %{
  actor_id: "agent-1",
  guild_id: "guild-1",
  channel_id: "123",
  metadata: %{attempt: 1}
})
```

Persist these envelopes to track state changes, emit downstream events, and
connect retry attempts to the original operation.
